  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Using the law to address illegal and unsustainable use of forest resources and promote more equitable forest and land governance</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The global challenge of deforestation requires strong and equitable governance systems, on both the demand and supply sides. ClientEarth&#x2019;s project promotes strong regulations across major producer and consumer markets, compelling behavioural change through legal compliance, increased rights for Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLCs) and incentives for deforestation-free products. ClientEarth&#x2019;s project supports national actors in Africa &#x2013; primarily civil society organisations (CSOs), IPLCs, women, and governments &#x2013; to engage in national law reform to enhance their long-term role in strong forest stewardship related to (i) forest-risk commodities (timber, cocoa and minerals) and (ii) carbon markets. ClientEarth&#x2019;s project will also engage with Chinese authorities, including law enforcement and environmental regulators, and large Chinese companies on the global deforestation footprint of China&#x2019;s overseas trade and investment. The project will leverage international engagement and national implementation of the Global Biodiversity Framework, to create momentum for stewardship of some of the world&#x2019;s most important remaining forests.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project pursues the following outcomes: 1. More effective, deliberative and accountable laws in forested countries; and 2. Improved policies and law enforcement by Chinese authorities related to China&apos;s overseas deforestation footprint</narrative>
  </description>
